Task 1: Configure Cross-Domain Trust with ipa trust-add
Subtask 1.1: Verify IdM Environment Prerequisites
First, let's ensure our IdM environment is properly configured for trust establishment.

Connect to your IdM server and verify the current status:
# Check IdM server status
sudo ipactl status

# Verify DNS configuration
sudo ipa dnszone-show example.com

# Check current realm information
sudo ipa config-show | grep -i realm
Install required trust packages if not already present:
# Install trust-related packages
sudo dnf install -y ipa-server-trust-ad samba-client

# Restart IdM services after package installation
sudo ipactl restart
Verify Kerberos configuration:
# Check Kerberos realm configuration
sudo cat /etc/krb5.conf

# Test Kerberos authentication
sudo kinit admin
sudo klist
Subtask 1.2: Prepare Active Directory Environment
Before establishing trust, ensure the AD environment is ready.

Verify AD domain information (perform on AD server):
# Check AD domain details
Get-ADDomain

# Verify DNS settings
nslookup example.com
nslookup ad.example.com
Create a trust user account in Active Directory:
# Create trust administrator account
New-ADUser -Name "trustadmin" -UserPrincipalName "trustadmin@ad.example.com" -AccountPassword (ConvertTo-SecureString "TrustPass123!" -AsPlainText -Force) -Enabled $true

# Add to Domain Admins group
Add-ADGroupMember -Identity "Domain Admins" -Members "trustadmin"
Subtask 1.3: Configure DNS for Trust Relationship
Proper DNS configuration is crucial for trust establishment.

Configure DNS forwarders on IdM server:
# Add DNS forwarder for AD domain
sudo ipa dnsforwardzone-add ad.example.com --forwarder=192.168.1.100 --forward-policy=only

# Verify DNS resolution
nslookup ad.example.com
nslookup _ldap._tcp.ad.example.com
Test DNS resolution from both directions:
# From IdM server, test AD DNS resolution
dig @192.168.1.100 ad.example.com
dig @192.168.1.100 _kerberos._tcp.ad.example.com SRV

# Verify reverse DNS
dig -x 192.168.1.100
Subtask 1.4: Establish Trust Using ipa trust-add
Now we'll create the actual trust relationship between IdM and AD.

Authenticate as IdM administrator:
# Obtain Kerberos ticket for admin user
sudo kinit admin
Add the trust relationship:
# Establish one-way trust with AD domain
sudo ipa trust-add ad.example.com --type=ad --admin=trustadmin --password

# You will be prompted for the AD administrator password
# Enter: TrustPass123!
Configure trust for two-way authentication (if required):
# Enable two-way trust
sudo ipa trust-mod ad.example.com --trust-type=bidirectional
Verify trust creation:
# List all configured trusts
sudo ipa trust-show ad.example.com

# Check trust status
sudo ipa trustdomain-find ad.example.com
Task 2: Verify Trust Status and Configure Trust Settings
Subtask 2.1: Validate Trust Establishment
Let's verify that the trust relationship is working correctly.

Check trust connectivity:
# Test trust connectivity
sudo ipa trust-fetch-domains ad.example.com

# Verify domain controller connectivity
sudo sss_cache -E
sudo systemctl restart sssd
Examine trust details:
# Display comprehensive trust information
sudo ipa trust-show ad.example.com --all

# Check trust domain information
sudo ipa trustdomain-show ad.example.com
Validate Kerberos trust:
# Test Kerberos cross-realm authentication
sudo kinit administrator@AD.EXAMPLE.COM

# Verify ticket-granting ticket
sudo klist
Subtask 2.2: Configure ID Ranges for Trust
Proper ID range configuration ensures unique user and group IDs across domains.

View current ID ranges:
# List all ID ranges
sudo ipa idrange-find

# Show specific trust ID range
sudo ipa idrange-show AD.EXAMPLE.COM_id_range
Modify ID range if necessary:
# Modify ID range base (if conflicts exist)
sudo ipa idrange-mod AD.EXAMPLE.COM_id_range --base-id=1200000 --range-size=200000
Configure default shell and home directory:
# Set default shell for AD users
sudo ipa config-mod --defaultshell=/bin/bash

# Configure home directory template
sudo ipa config-mod --homedirectory=/home/%u
Subtask 2.3: Configure SSSD for Trust Integration
SSSD must be properly configured to handle cross-domain authentication.

Update SSSD configuration:
# Backup current SSSD configuration
sudo cp /etc/sssd/sssd.conf /etc/sssd/sssd.conf.backup

# Edit SSSD configuration
sudo vi /etc/sssd/sssd.conf
Add trust-specific configuration:
[domain/example.com]
# Existing configuration...
subdomain_inherit = parent_id
subdomain_homedir = /home/%d/%u
default_shell = /bin/bash
krb5_store_password_if_offline = True
cache_credentials = True
krb5_realm = EXAMPLE.COM
Restart and verify SSSD:
# Restart SSSD service
sudo systemctl restart sssd

# Clear SSSD cache
sudo sss_cache -E

# Verify SSSD status
sudo systemctl status sssd
Task 3: Test Cross-Domain Logins Between IdM and AD
Subtask 3.1: Test AD User Authentication on IdM Client
Now we'll test whether AD users can authenticate on IdM-joined systems.

Prepare the client system:
# Ensure client is properly joined to IdM
sudo ipa-client-install --unattended --principal=admin --password=AdminPass123

# Verify client enrollment
sudo ipa host-show client.example.com
Test AD user lookup:
# Look up AD user information
getent passwd administrator@ad.example.com
getent group "domain users@ad.example.com"

# Test ID resolution
id administrator@ad.example.com
Attempt AD user login:
# Test SSH login with AD credentials
ssh administrator@ad.example.com@localhost

# Alternative: Test local login
sudo login
# Username: administrator@ad.example.com
# Password: [AD user password]
Subtask 3.2: Configure and Test Group Mapping
Group mapping allows AD groups to be used for authorization in IdM environment.

Create external group mapping:
# Create external group for AD Domain Admins
sudo ipa group-add --desc="AD Domain Admins" ad_domain_admins --external

# Add AD group to external group
sudo ipa group-add-member ad_domain_admins --external="Domain Admins@ad.example.com"
Create POSIX group and add external group:
# Create POSIX group
sudo ipa group-add --desc="Administrators" administrators

# Add external group to POSIX group
sudo ipa group-add-member administrators --groups=ad_domain_admins
Test group membership:
# Verify group membership for AD user
groups administrator@ad.example.com

# Check group information
getent group administrators
Subtask 3.3: Configure SUDO Rules for AD Users
Enable AD users to use sudo privileges through IdM SUDO rules.

Create SUDO rule for AD administrators:
# Create SUDO rule
sudo ipa sudorule-add --desc="AD Administrators SUDO" ad_admin_sudo

# Add administrators group to SUDO rule
sudo ipa sudorule-add-user ad_admin_sudo --groups=administrators

# Add SUDO commands
sudo ipa sudorule-add-allow-command ad_admin_sudo --sudocmds=ALL

# Add target hosts
sudo ipa sudorule-add-host ad_admin_sudo --hosts=client.example.com
Test SUDO access:
# Login as AD user and test sudo
ssh administrator@ad.example.com@client.example.com
sudo whoami
sudo systemctl status sshd
Subtask 3.4: Verify Cross-Domain Authentication Logs
Monitor and verify authentication events in system logs.

Check authentication logs:
# Monitor secure log for authentication events
sudo tail -f /var/log/secure

# Check SSSD logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/sssd/sssd_example.com.log

# Review Kerberos logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/krb5kdc.log
Verify successful authentication events:
# Search for successful AD user logins
sudo grep "authentication.*ad.example.com" /var/log/secure

# Check SSSD domain logs
sudo grep -i "trust" /var/log/sssd/sssd_example.com.log
Troubleshooting Common Issues
DNS Resolution Problems
If trust establishment fails due to DNS issues:

# Verify DNS configuration
sudo ipa dnsconfig-show

# Test DNS resolution manually
nslookup _kerberos._tcp.ad.example.com
nslookup _ldap._tcp.ad.example.com

# Restart DNS services
sudo systemctl restart named-pkcs11
Kerberos Authentication Failures
For Kerberos-related trust issues:

# Check Kerberos configuration
sudo ipa-adtrust-install --add-sids

# Verify time synchronization
sudo chrony sources -v

# Test cross-realm authentication
sudo kinit -V administrator@AD.EXAMPLE.COM
SSSD Cache Issues
If user lookups fail:

# Clear SSSD cache completely
sudo sss_cache -E

# Restart SSSD with debug logging
sudo sssd -i -d 9

# Check SSSD domain status
sudo sssctl domain-status example.com
Verification and Testing
Complete Trust Verification Checklist
Trust Status Verification:
# Comprehensive trust check
sudo ipa trust-show ad.example.com --all
sudo ipa trustdomain-find ad.example.com
sudo sssctl domain-list
User Authentication Test:
# Test multiple AD users
getent passwd administrator@ad.example.com
getent passwd testuser@ad.example.com
id administrator@ad.example.com
Group Membership Verification:
# Verify group mappings
groups administrator@ad.example.com
getent group administrators
sudo ipa group-show administrators --all
